## 4.5 Labelled recalibration: what it costs (Q3)

At k = 10, the required rank exceeds k, so no finite 90% interval exists with separate α/2 corrections. For k = 20, 40, 80 and 160, mean coverage was {rc_cov_20}, {rc_cov_40}, {rc_cov_80} and {rc_cov_160}. These match the theoretical expected values ({rc_theory_20}, {rc_theory_40}, {rc_theory_80}, {rc_theory_160}) for every transition (range {rc_min_160}–{rc_max_20}). This agreement is expected under exchangeability and is not an empirical finding.

The informative quantity is width. Mean widths were {rc_w_20}, {rc_w_40}, {rc_w_80} and {rc_w_160} years, against {rc_width_src} at the source (Figure 2; Table 3). At k = 40 the per-transition width ratio to the source had median {rc_ratio40_median} and range {rc_ratio40_min}–{rc_ratio40_max}. Standardising before recalibrating gave the same coverage with narrower intervals ({rch_w_20}, {rch_w_40}, {rch_w_80} and {rch_w_160} years; median ratio {rch_ratio40_median}, maximum {rch_ratio40_max}).

An initial analysis drew recalibration subjects from training and calibration subjects and used a quantile one rank above the formula in §3.6. It reported non-monotone coverage (0.948 at k = 40); this was an artefact of the rank rule. Removing training subjects from the pool changed coverage by {rc_pool_eff_40} at k = 40.

