## 4.6 Label-free drift monitoring

On raw target features, the MMD alarmed on every batch of every transition. That included the two harmless FreeSurfer 5.3↔6.0 transitions, which the KS test on predicted ages flagged at most {mon_ctrl_ks_raw_max} of the time.

After per-pipeline standardisation, the MMD alarm rate fell to a mean of {mon_mmd_std_detect_mean_nc} (median {mon_mmd_std_detect_median_nc}; maximum {mon_std_detect_max}), and the KS rate to {mon_ks_std_detect_mean_nc}. The observed false-alarm rate was {mon_far_mmd_std_mean} (per-transition maximum {mon_far_max}). Yet {mon_n_harmful_ci} of the 18 transitions had a coverage loss whose CI excluded zero, and none of them reached a majority-alarm rate. For example, FreeSurfer 6.0 → CIVET lost {fs60_civet_d_std_vs_src} with an alarm rate of {mon_fs60_civet_detect} (Figure 4). Alarm rates rose with batch size, from {mon_b25_std} at 25 to {mon_b100_std} (maximum {mon_b100_std_max}) at 100. The relation between alarm rate and loss across transitions was weak (Spearman ρ = {mon_spearman_mmd_std_detect_vs_d_std}; descriptive).

